# How the selfie lays out a tree.
#
# The root is at the top. A stem's child is right below it. A fork's children
# are k rows down and k columns to either side, at the ends of diagonal edges,
# with k as small as keeps them apart: at least one empty column between the
# two subtrees in every row, and between neighbouring rows.
#
# [lay t] is △ forks outline. forks holds every fork's k: for a leaf it is [],
# for a stem its child's, and for a fork △ (△ left right) k, left and right
# its children's. The outline is t's shape: for each row below the root, △ l r,
# how much further the leftmost pixel reaches left (l) and the rightmost
# reaches right (r) than in the row above. l and r are numbers as church makes
# them: [n f g s] applies f n times, or g −n times.

# Two outlines side by side, their roots in one column, overlap by how far the
# left one's right edge is right of the right one's left edge (0 in the same
# column). The need is the most they overlap in any row, or between
# neighbouring rows. Walking down both, the state is △ g n: n the need so far,
# and g how far the overlap now is below it. [_up s] is s with the overlap one
# more, [_down s] one less.
_up = Pair.match $ \g \n △ (△ (Pair.create 0 (Snat.succ n)) (\g Pair.create g n)) _ g
_down = Pair.match $ \g \n Pair.create (Snat.succ g) n
# [_step ud ra s lb]: s after a row in which the left outline's right edge
# moves ra columns right and the right outline's left edge lb columns left;
# [ud x s] is s with the overlap grown by x. A rise is counted before a fall,
# so each edge's new place is also measured against the other's old one: that
# is the "between neighbouring rows".
_step = \ud \ra \s \lb
  (\a \b △ (△ (\s a (b s)) (\_ \s b (a s))) _ (ra (\_ false) (\_ true) false) s) (ud lb) (ud ra)
# A fork's k from the need: the smallest with 2k ≥ need + 2. It is taken from
# ks, the list 1, 2, …, 256, so that forks with the same k share one number.
_half = fix $ \h △ (△ Pair.fst (\p \ks △ (△ (Pair.fst (Pair.snd ks)) (\q h q (Pair.snd ks))) _ p)) _
_ks_step = Pair.match $ \v \l Pair.create (triage _ id _ v) (List.cons v l)
_ks = \iter \church (\n Pair.snd (church n _ks_step _ (Pair.create n []))) (iter 0 256 Snat.succ)
# One walk down both children's outlines finds the need and merges them into
# the fork's outline below its diagonals: the left edge from the left child,
# the right edge from the right child. Where the shorter one ends, the longer
# one's edge takes over that side, and that row's move is from where the
# shorter one's edge last was: −2k, as the children are 2k apart, plus how far
# the two edges have drifted apart on the way down, plus the longer one's move.
#
# [_nm_end s f]: △ k rows, k from the final state s, and rows f m2k, with
# m2k = −2k.
_nm_end = \iter \dec \church \s \f (\k Pair.create k (f (church k dec _ (church k dec _ 0)))) (_half (Pair.snd s) (_ks iter church))
# [_jsum s f g j]: s, then for each △ x y of j, x steps by f and y steps by g:
# with f one up and g one down, s plus the sum of x − y.
_jsum = \s \f \g fix (\r triage s _ (\p \l Pair.match (\x \y x f g (y g f (r l))) p))
# [_nm_over … e j rest mk s], … being the functions it is passed: where only
# the longer outline goes on. Its row is mk of its move from the shorter one's
# edge (see above: e its own move, j the moves so far on that side), then
# rest, its rows below. The state s takes e too, which measures that row
# against the shorter one's last.
_nm_over = \ud \inc \dec \church \fin \e \j \rest \mk \s fin (ud e s) (\m2k List.cons (mk (church (e inc dec (_jsum m2k inc dec j)))) rest)
# [_nm_next … w jl jr ca la ra hb cb s]: where both go on, the left child's
# row being △ la ra and the right child's hb: the merged row la rb, left edge
# from the left child and right edge from the right child, then the walk w on
# from the rows below.
_nm_next = \ud \w \jl \jr \ca \la \ra \hb \cb \s Pair.match (\lb \rb
    Pair.match (\k \rows Pair.create k (List.cons (Pair.create la rb) rows))
      (w (_step ud ra s lb) (List.cons (Pair.create lb la) jl) (List.cons (Pair.create ra rb) jr) ca cb))
  hb
# The walk, [w s jl jr ca cb]: s the state; ca and cb the two outlines' rows
# still to come; jl and jr the moves so far on the left and right side, the
# latest first, each △ x y with x the move of the child that would take over
# that side and y the other's.
_nm = \iter \inc \dec \church (\ud
  (\fin (\over fix $ \w \s \jl \jr \ca \cb
    △ (△ (△ (△ (\s fin s (\_ [])) _) (\hb \cb \s Pair.match (\lb \rb over lb jl cb (\x Pair.create x rb) s) hb)) _)
      (\ha \ca \cb \s Pair.match (\la \ra
        △ (△ (\ra over ra jr ca (Pair.create la)) _) (\hb \cb \ra _nm_next ud w jl jr ca la ra hb cb) cb ra s)
        ha)
      ca cb s)
    (_nm_over ud inc dec church fin))
  (_nm_end iter dec church)) (\x x _up _down)
lay = \iter \inc \dec \church
  (\nm \diag fix $ \r △ (△ (Pair.create [] []) (\c Pair.match (\a \rows Pair.create a (List.cons (Pair.create Pico.Matrix.Selfie.zero Pico.Matrix.Selfie.zero) rows)) (r c)))
    (\a \b Pair.match (\aa \ca Pair.match (\ab \cb
      Pair.match (\k \rows Pair.create (Pair.create (Pair.create aa ab) k) (diag k rows)) (nm (Pair.create 0 0) [] [] ca cb))
      (r b)) (r a)))
  (_nm iter inc dec church) (\k church k (List.cons (Pair.create const const)) _)
